<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tuʻi Manuʻa Elisala</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1909">1909</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tuʻi Manuʻa Elisala was the paramount chief of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manua-islands">Manuʻa Islands</a> at the eastern end of Samoa at the turn of the 20th century. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripartite-convention-1899">Tripartite Convention of 1899</a> assigned eastern Samoa to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, he signed the deed of cession of Manuʻa on 16 July 1904. He was the last holder of the title recognised as ruler before it lapsed under American administration.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tui-manua-elisala.jpg" alt="Tuʻi Manuʻa Elisala" width="320" height="438" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: unknown, uploaded by Taulapapa at en.wiki · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Elisala.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
